{"claims":[{"excerpt":"Who Counts as Unemployed?","claim":"The title frames unemployment as a category with criteria for who is counted.","category":"interpretation","label":"F0 faithful","evidence":"Under the Current Population Survey, an unemployed person was not employed during the survey reference week, was available for work except for temporary illness, and made an active job search effort in the four-week period ending with that week or was temporarily laid off and expected recall.","narrative":"Explain who is counted as unemployed—and therefore included in the unemployment-rate denominator—through two contrasting situations: a temporarily laid-off person expecting recall and someone who only browsed job listings.","narrativeRelation":"supports","origin":"propagated","explanation":"The title is a question, not an additional factual assertion; its framing is consistent with the source and narrative.","materiality":"Low; it introduces the subject without adding a factual detail."},{"excerpt":"The unemployment rate is calculated using the number of unemployed people in relation to the combined number of employed and unemployed people","claim":"The unemployment rate uses the number of unemployed people relative to the combined number of employed and unemployed people.","category":"quantity","label":"F0 faithful","evidence":"The unemployment rate divides unemployed people by the sum of employed and unemployed people.","narrative":"The rate’s denominator is the number of employed people plus unemployed people.","narrativeRelation":"supports","origin":"propagated","explanation":"The stated numerator and denominator match the source and narrative.","materiality":"High; this is the definition of the rate’s denominator."},{"excerpt":"not everyone who does not have a job.","claim":"The unemployment-rate denominator is not everyone without a job.","category":"scope","label":"F0 faithful","evidence":"The unemployment rate divides unemployed people by the sum of employed and unemployed people.","narrative":"rather than by everyone without a job.","narrativeRelation":"supports","origin":"propagated","explanation":"The source defines the denominator as employed plus unemployed people, not all people without jobs.","materiality":"High; it distinguishes unemployment from the broader condition of not having a job."},{"excerpt":"Whether someone is counted as unemployed depends on their circumstances during specific survey periods.","claim":"Unemployment classification depends on a person's circumstances during survey reference periods.","category":"chronology","label":"F0 faithful","evidence":"Under the Current Population Survey, an unemployed person was not employed during the survey reference week, was available for work except for temporary illness, and made an active job search effort in the four-week period ending with that week or was temporarily laid off and expected recall.","narrative":"Clarify that availability and the survey reference periods also matter","narrativeRelation":"supports","origin":"propagated","explanation":"The source specifies both a reference week and a four-week search period; the article's general wording preserves that timing distinction.","materiality":"Moderate; the survey periods determine which circumstances count."},{"excerpt":"a person must not have been employed during the reference week","claim":"A person must not have been employed during the reference week to qualify as unemployed.","category":"chronology","label":"F0 faithful","evidence":"an unemployed person was not employed during the survey reference week","narrative":"the person must not have been employed during the reference week","narrativeRelation":"supports","origin":"propagated","explanation":"This matches the source's employment-status condition.","materiality":"High; employment during the reference week affects classification."},{"excerpt":"must have been available for work—with an exception for temporary illness","claim":"A person must be available for work, except when temporarily ill.","category":"scope","label":"F0 faithful","evidence":"was available for work except for temporary illness","narrative":"must have been available for work, except for temporary illness","narrativeRelation":"supports","origin":"propagated","explanation":"The availability requirement and temporary-illness exception match the source.","materiality":"High; availability is a qualification condition."},{"excerpt":"must have actively searched for work during the four-week period ending with that week.","claim":"A person generally must have actively searched for work during the four weeks ending with the reference week.","category":"chronology","label":"F0 faithful","evidence":"made an active job search effort in the four-week period ending with that week","narrative":"Active search must fall within the four-week period ending with the reference week","narrativeRelation":"supports","origin":"propagated","explanation":"The article accurately states the active-search requirement and its period; the following sentence supplies the layoff exception.","materiality":"High; both the activity and its timing affect classification."},{"excerpt":"A person on temporary layoff who expects to be recalled may qualify without actively searching, provided the other conditions are met.","claim":"A person on temporary layoff who expects recall may qualify without active job search if the other conditions are met.","category":"scope","label":"F0 faithful","evidence":"or was temporarily laid off and expected recall","narrative":"The first may qualify without an active search","narrativeRelation":"supports","origin":"propagated","explanation":"The article correctly presents expected recall from temporary layoff as an alternative to active search while retaining the other conditions.","materiality":"High; this is a specific exception to the usual search requirement."},{"excerpt":"Someone whose only job-search activity was browsing listings does not meet the active-search requirement.","claim":"Browsing listings alone does not count as active job search.","category":"technical","label":"F0 faithful","evidence":"Passive-only job search does not count as active search.","narrative":"someone who only browsed job listings. The first may qualify without an active search; passive browsing alone does not meet the active-search requirement.","narrativeRelation":"supports","origin":"propagated","explanation":"Browsing listings alone is accurately treated as passive-only search.","materiality":"Moderate; it clarifies which search activity satisfies the requirement."},{"excerpt":"availability, employment status during the reference week, and the type and timing of job-search activity determine whether a person is included among the unemployed","claim":"Availability, reference-week employment status, and the type and timing of job search determine whether a person is counted as unemployed.","category":"scope","label":"F0 faithful","evidence":"an unemployed person was not employed during the survey reference week, was available for work except for temporary illness, and made an active job search effort in the four-week period ending with that week or was temporarily laid off and expected recall. Passive-only job search does not count as active search.","narrative":"availability and the survey reference periods also matter","narrativeRelation":"supports","origin":"propagated","explanation":"This summarizes the source's classification conditions and passive-search distinction without adding a criterion.","materiality":"High; it gathers the conditions that determine classification."},{"excerpt":"and therefore in the unemployment rate’s denominator.","claim":"Being counted as unemployed places a person in the unemployment rate's denominator.","category":"other","label":"F0 faithful","evidence":"The unemployment rate divides unemployed people by the sum of employed and unemployed people.","narrative":"included in the unemployment-rate denominator","narrativeRelation":"supports","origin":"propagated","explanation":"The relationship follows directly from the source's definition of the denominator.","materiality":"High; it links the classification criteria to the rate."}]}

